bgload {$strS[1000]}.jpg 98
if intF[1113] == 1
	if strS[1000] < "C"
		setimg {$strS[1004]}.png -7 -7
	fi
fi
text We've traveled far to find scrap today.
text We stand at the top of a hill, looking out at the horizon.
text The earth stretches on endlessly.
text It goes out as far as the eye can see.
text What could be on the other end of it?
text I compare myself to the grass growing next to me.
text But I can't tell for sure with this tiny body of mine.
text ..."Look."
text I turn around to face the voice, and I see her holding an animal to her chest.
text So there WERE living creatures in this world apart from me.
text It's covered in fluffy white hair and has two curly horns, one by each ear.
text I don't like it.
text It doesn't seem to have any interest in people ─ not that I'm a person.
text It's as if it doesn't even notice us.
text Still, she enjoys playing with it, even if it doesn't react.
text She pats its head and tummy as she holds it...
text ...all the while slowly climbing the hill.
text I follow after her.
text At the top of one hill, we find a herd of these animals.
text While they're all of different sizes, both big and small, they're the same species.
text The girl places the animal she's holding down on the ground.
text It walks toward its herd, not even looking back at her once.
text Seeing this makes me think...
text They must not have "hearts."
text They just exist.
text If it never had any interest in her, of course it wouldn't turn back or feel sad about parting ways.
text Still, the only living creatures in this world besides her are those things.
text That must be why she enjoys interacting with them.
text As I watch the animals, I see them unemotionally tear flowers out from the ground they're sprouting on and chew them up.
text If those things keep multiplying, this green field will quickly become a wasteland.
text No matter how much she loves them, she surely wouldn't want that to happen.
text I place the junk I'm carrying down on the ground then charge into the pack of animals to drive them off.
text At least, I try to do that; but they give up without a fight, change locations, and begin to eat more grass.
text My actions were meaningless.
text ..."What's wrong? Are you mad?"
text I hear the girl's voice behind me.
text No. I'm sad.
text ..."Do you want me to pat your head?"
text Well... Yes, I do.
text I sit still, looking at the now-upturned soil.
text Does she not feel anything when she sees nature this despoiled?
text Not sadness or loneliness?
text She crouches down and pats my head. I can tell by the shadows.
text ..."There's just nothing we can do about this."
text Those are her words to me. She goes on:
text ..."Ah, things are always changing..."
text ..."That's the only thought I can come to."
music ~
